{"subjectId":"c","id":"function-pointers","title":"Function Pointers & Callbacks","group":"Pointers","order":27,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A function’s name decays to its address. Storing that in a <em>function pointer</em> lets you pass behaviour as data — callbacks, dispatch tables, plug-in strategies, <code>qsort</code> comparators.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Syntax</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int (*op)(int, int) = add;        /* pointer to function taking (int,int) returning int */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", op(3, 4));\n    op = &amp;mul;                        /* &amp; is optional */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", (*op)(3, 4));      /* explicit * is optional too */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7\n12</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>int (*op)(int, int)</code> — the parentheses matter. <code>int *op(int, int)</code> would declare a <em>function</em> returning <code>int *</code>. A <code>typedef</code> makes it readable: <code>typedef int (*binop)(int, int);</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i>Callbacks: qsort</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint cmp_int_asc(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    int x = *(const int *)a, y = *(const int *)b;\n    return (x &gt; y) - (x &lt; y);         /* avoids overflow of x - y */\n}\n\nint cmp_str(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    return strcmp(*(const char *const *)a, *(const char *const *)b);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int nums[] = {42, -7, 19, 0, 3};\n    qsort(nums, 5, sizeof nums[0], cmp_int_asc);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) printf(\"%d \", nums[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n\n    const char *words[] = {\"pear\", \"apple\", \"fig\"};\n    qsort(words, 3, sizeof words[0], cmp_str);\n    printf(\"%s %s %s\\n\", words[0], words[1], words[2]);\n\n    int key = 19;\n    int *hit = bsearch(&amp;key, nums, 5, sizeof nums[0], cmp_int_asc);\n    printf(\"19 at index %td\\n\", hit - nums);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>-7 0 3 19 42\napple fig pear\n19 at index 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>return x - y;</code> in a comparator overflows for large values of opposite sign (<code>INT_MIN - 1</code>). Use <code>(x &gt; y) - (x &lt; y)</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-function\"></i>Your own higher-order function</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef int (*transform_fn)(int);\n\nint square(int x) { return x * x; }\nint negate(int x) { return -x; }\n\nvoid map(int *a, int n, transform_fn f) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) a[i] = f(a[i]);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int v[] = {1, 2, 3};\n    map(v, 3, square);\n    map(v, 3, negate);\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", v[0], v[1], v[2]);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>-1 -4 -9</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Dispatch tables</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ndouble add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }\ndouble sub(double a, double b) { return a - b; }\ndouble mul(double a, double b) { return a * b; }\ndouble dvd(double a, double b) { return a / b; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    struct { char sym; double (*fn)(double, double); } ops[] = {\n        {'+', add}, {'-', sub}, {'*', mul}, {'/', dvd},\n    };\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++)\n        printf(\"8 %c 2 = %g\\n\", ops[i].sym, ops[i].fn(8, 2));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>8 + 2 = 10\n8 - 2 = 6\n8 * 2 = 16\n8 / 2 = 4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>Callbacks with context</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n/* C has no closures: pass state through a void * \"user data\" pointer */\nvoid for_each(const int *a, int n, void (*visit)(int, void *), void *ctx) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) visit(a[i], ctx);\n}\n\nvoid accumulate(int x, void *ctx) { *(long *)ctx += x; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {5, 10, 20};\n    long total = 0;\n    for_each(a, 3, accumulate, &amp;total);\n    printf(\"total = %ld\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>total = 35</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you declare a function that returns a function pointer?</summary><div><p>Directly: <code>int (*get_op(char c))(int, int);</code> — <code>get_op</code> takes a <code>char</code> and returns a pointer to a function <code>(int, int) -&gt; int</code>. With a typedef it is readable: <code>typedef int (*binop)(int, int); binop get_op(char c);</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are function pointers used for in real systems?</summary><div><p>Callbacks (<code>qsort</code>, <code>bsearch</code>, <code>atexit</code>, <code>signal</code>, event handlers), dispatch/jump tables for interpreters and state machines, plug-in interfaces (a struct of function pointers is C's version of a vtable — e.g. Linux <code>struct file_operations</code>), and dynamic loading with <code>dlsym</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}